00:21:57,960 --> 00:21:59,440
 Come on, hurry up, you guys. Come on.

240
00:21:59,540 --> 00:22:01,200
 Strangers, they're coming up over the hill.

241
00:22:05,130 --> 00:22:10,370
 Let's give a dose of real civilization to these savages.

242
00:22:11,070 --> 00:22:14,440
 A small fee in gold will introduce happiness and progress.

243
00:22:15,300 --> 00:22:18,680
 All right, my fine-feathered friends, show us what you're made of.

244
00:22:19,150 --> 00:22:19,410
 Forward!

245
00:22:19,410 --> 00:22:39,510
 In August of the year 1521, Cortes completed his conquest of Mexico.

246
00:22:41,050 --> 00:22:43,330
 In that year, there were 12 million Indians in the land.

247
00:22:43,870 --> 00:22:46,360
 A few years later, Cortes had butchered half of them.

248
00:22:47,070 --> 00:22:49,390
 And in Peru, Pizarro left an even bloodier trail.

249
00:22:56,100 --> 00:22:59,540
 The Conquistadores brought Spain vast territories and wealth.

250
00:23:02,850 --> 00:23:05,010
 1521, war broke out again with France.

251
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:15,420
 1524, in a Germany splintered into 400 states, Luther's spark ignited the flame of rebellion against the empire and the privileges of the church.

252
00:23:15,970 --> 00:23:17,850
 I've just had an idea. Watch this.

253
00:23:17,850 --> 00:23:21,910
 What would you say to this wheel fitted with paddles and run by water?

254
00:23:21,910 --> 00:23:26,640
 Well, fine. Say no more. I see you're bored to tears.

255
00:23:26,640 --> 00:23:29,760
 Let's forget I said anything, I won't bring it up again.

256
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:35,440
 But I've got another idea. Why don't we go down to the port and see if the children have returned?

257
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:36,660
 Ah, that's a good idea.

258
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:41,630
 In 1530, at the height of his power, Charles V was crowned emperor by Pope Clement VII.

259
00:23:41,630 --> 00:23:46,780
 But the Reformation was gaining ground. It had reached Geneva, where Calvin was continuing William Farrell's work.

260
00:23:46,780 --> 00:23:51,390
 The years go by, and still no sign of the youngsters.

261
00:23:52,200 --> 00:23:55,700
 1537, Jacques Cartier discovers and explores Canada.

262
00:23:56,070 --> 00:23:58,370
 The banner of France as the first waves over Mount Royal.

263
00:24:01,690 --> 00:24:05,920
 Wars, victories, and defeats multiply between Spain and France.

264
00:24:07,590 --> 00:24:10,770
 Don't tell me he didn't like the land of gold.

265
00:24:11,660 --> 00:24:14,300
 My native land! I'm home! I'm home!

266
00:24:14,650 --> 00:24:18,410
 In 1543, the Frenchman Ambroise Paré invented the ligation of arteries.

267
00:24:18,690 --> 00:24:21,110
 In Flanders, André Vassal dissected the human body.

268
00:24:21,450 --> 00:24:23,610
 And Copernicus finally published his treatise on the Earth's road fish.

269
00:24:23,610 --> 00:24:25,070
 Lord, sir, I've got marbles to show you.

270
00:24:25,890 --> 00:24:28,400
 I'm certain you'll be most interested.

271
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:31,260
 No.

272
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,370
 Wait, see, here. Potatoes?

273
00:24:34,370 --> 00:24:34,850
 No.

274
00:24:34,850 --> 00:24:37,630
 Green beans?

275
00:24:37,630 --> 00:24:39,870
 Potatoes?

276
00:24:39,870 --> 00:24:41,370
 Chocolate?

277
00:24:41,370 --> 00:24:43,030
 Gold. Got any?

278
00:24:43,030 --> 00:24:46,240
 No, but I've got much more. Look at this marvel, it's a turkey.

279
00:24:46,240 --> 00:24:47,740
 Not interested.

280
00:24:47,740 --> 00:24:50,190
 Come now, come now, come now.

281
00:24:50,190 --> 00:24:51,260
 Tobacco.

282
00:24:51,260 --> 00:24:53,560
 Look!

283
00:24:56,630 --> 00:25:04,410
 One moment. My name is van der Hoot from Amsterdam. I'm a merchant. I'll take everything you've shown.

284
00:25:04,410 --> 00:25:05,090
 Here.

285
00:25:05,090 --> 00:25:08,800
 That's not enough.

286
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:09,920
 I'll forward the rest.

287
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:14,170
 In 1545, after a long delay, a council met at Trent.

288
00:25:14,170 --> 00:25:21,380
 Figure in all this.

289
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:22,530
 Ah, peak man!

290
00:25:23,350 --> 00:25:25,950
 Now calm down, I've still got something you're sure to like.

291
00:25:26,650 --> 00:25:26,910
 Here.

292
00:25:27,330 --> 00:25:28,930
 Now then, why didn't you say so?

293
00:25:29,220 --> 00:25:31,140
 See, everything irons itself out.

294
00:25:31,420 --> 00:25:32,540
 Dutchman takes the rest.

295
00:25:32,640 --> 00:25:33,200
 There you go.

296
00:25:34,870 --> 00:25:37,930
 Both Francis I and Henry VIII died in 1547,

297
00:25:38,170 --> 00:25:39,950
 and Charles V beat the Protestant League

298
00:25:39,950 --> 00:25:42,040
 in the insignificant Battle of Milburg.

299
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:44,080
 Children never come back.

300
00:25:44,630 --> 00:25:48,000
 In 1553, an exhausted Charles retires to Brussels.

301
00:25:49,540 --> 00:25:51,170
 But why should I only get half?

302
00:25:51,250 --> 00:25:52,290
 That ain't fair, I say.

303
00:25:54,410 --> 00:25:55,840
 Line up, line up.

304
00:25:55,840 --> 00:26:00,250
 Died of Augsburg, recognized the Protestant religion in late 1554.

305
00:26:00,250 --> 00:26:03,930
 Maybe it's our lucky day, who knows, the next boat might just bring our boys home.

306
00:26:03,930 --> 00:26:12,250
 Charles V abdicated the throne and Philip II took over.

307
00:26:12,250 --> 00:26:15,330
 Nothing yet, I can't take much more of this.

308
00:26:15,330 --> 00:26:22,650
 1558 saw the death of Charles V.

309
00:26:22,650 --> 00:26:26,370
 He claimed to have fought not for fame, but for the good of his states and subjects.

310
00:26:26,370 --> 00:26:29,500
 That same year, Elizabeth became Queen of England.

311
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:32,700
 And in 1559, she made peace with France.

312
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:36,090
 Philip II had won the war, but his country had suffered badly.

313
00:26:36,090 --> 00:26:45,330
 1571, Spain takes heart after her victory at Lepanto.

314
00:26:45,900 --> 00:26:49,020
 One young soldier lost a hand in battle, not his writing hand.

315
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:50,340
 His name was Cervantes.

316
00:26:50,980 --> 00:26:55,320
 1588, the invincible armada, symbol of Spain's golden age, sailed for England.

317
00:26:55,900 --> 00:26:57,660
 What would survive Valdez' grandeur?

318
00:26:58,050 --> 00:26:59,730
 The impressive Escorial.

319
00:27:00,740 --> 00:27:03,850
 Lope de Vega's picture of the glories and contradictions of his era.

320
00:27:04,390 --> 00:27:06,380
 The pure faith of St. Teresa of Avila.

321
00:27:06,760 --> 00:27:07,380
 El Greco.

322
00:27:07,380 --> 00:27:11,930
 And the one-armed warrior, Cervantes, gave us the legendary Don Quixote.

